# GraphQL Security: Query Depth, Complexity, and DoS Risks

## Security Risks

### Depth-Based DoS Attacks

Deeply nested queries can cause denial of service by exhausting server resources:

> "Even when the N+1 problem has been remediated through batched requests to underlying data sources, overly nested fields may still place excessive load on server resources and impact API performance."

The documentation provides this example of a problematic deeply nested query:

```graphql
query {
  hero {
    name
    friends {
      name
      friends {
        name
        friends {
          name
          friends {
            name
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

### Breadth-Based DoS Attacks

Queries can be exploited through field aliases and batching to multiply requests:

> "Even though the overall depth of this query is shallow, the underlying data source for the API will still have to handle a large number of requests to resolve data for the aliased `hero` field."

Example attack pattern:
```graphql
query {
  viewer {
    friends1: friends(limit: 1) { name }
    friends2: friends(limit: 2) { name }
    # ... friends100 ...
  }
}
```

## Recommended Mitigations

### 1. Depth Limiting

> "For this reason, it's a good idea to limit the maximum depth of fields that a single operation can have... it's recommended to apply a separate smaller limit to how deeply lists can be nested."

### 2. Breadth and Batch Limiting

> "there should also be guardrails in place to limit the number of top-level fields and field aliases included in a single operation... there should be an upper limit on the total number of queries allowed in a single batch."

### 3. Query Complexity Analysis

> "By applying weights to the types and fields in a schema, you can estimate the cost of incoming requests using the technique known as _query complexity analysis_. If the combination of fields included in a request exceeds a maximum allowable cost per request, you may choose to reject the request outright."

### 4. Rate Limiting

> "applying rate limits within the business logic layer is generally recommended" since GraphQL doesn't allow servers to know in advance which fields will be expensive to resolve.
